Tom:

Here is the additions to your R code that will implement the histogram
scaling as described by Prof. Buckland.  I have made use of objects you
had already defined (such as 'breaks' and 'point.hist')

nbands <- 5 # number of distance bands or intervals
k <- 30 # number of points
height.er <http://height.er> <- numeric(nbands)
for ( i in 1:nbands ) {
    annulus.area <- k * pi * (breaks[i+1]^2 - breaks[i]^2)
    apparent <- point.hist$counts[i] / annulus.area
    height.er <http://height.er>[i] <- apparent / 0.0070822 # constant is density estimate
(m^2) from Distance
}

you can then finish off the detection function figure you had started with

curve(detection.fn, from=0, to=w, lwd=2, col="red", add=F,
    ylim=c(0,1), xlab="radial detection distance (m)")

i <- seq(1:nbands)
segments(breaks[i], height.er <http://height.er>[i], breaks[i+1], height.er <http://height.er>[i])
segments(breaks[i], 0, breaks[i], height.er <http://height.er>[i])
segments(breaks[i+1], height.er <http://height.er>[i], breaks[i+1], height.er <http://height.er>[i+1])
segments(breaks[length(breaks)], height.er <http://height.er>[nbands],
breaks[length(breaks)],0)

